  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the real agentic AI threat is not a single sci-fi breakthrough but two ordinary, compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can work autonomously, per METR's time-horizon data) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, illustrated by Anthropic's disclosed GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code). Its central claim is that attackers don't pay list price: stolen cloud credentials enable \"LLMjacking\" (a term coined by Sysdig), so the victim pays the token bill, and price or access gates fail because they filter buyers rather than tenants. The author also uses the probability formula 1 − (1 − p)^n to show that cheap, repeated attempts make low per-attempt success rates dangerous in aggregate. The tone is an analytical, opinionated essay with a persuasive, slightly polemical edge, aimed at engineering teams and security or platform decision-makers who dismiss agentic threats as hype. Recurring framing devices include the Terminator franchise as an extended metaphor (Kyle Reese's \"absolutely will not stop\" line, \"Skynet is the villain, the robot is an API call,\" \"the meter is running\") and the product-of-curves model, with \"capability scales as duration × breadth\" as a key formula.",
